y representative survey and we  still see them neck-and-neck at about  the same but the same percentage points  how did President Obama do in full it  depends it didn't look really good for  President Obama about 48  said they disapprove of his job  performance as president with about  forty six percent saying they do approve  so it's pretty evenly divided between  those who are happy and those who are  unhappy with his job performance but I  would say almost more importantly the  disapproval is with Congress eighty  percent said that they are not happy  with the way things are going in  Congress well Republicans and Democrats  are the ones who make up the majority of  Congress do you see any hope for a third  party candidate well again that depends  there's a couple of things that might  point us in that direction first is we  know that we noticed that the most  likely responds when people were asked  what political party they identify with  was to say they're an independent  thirty-eight percent that I'm not going  to choose a political party now of  course most of those independents have a  pretty consistent partisan preference  but there's a reason why they're  choosing to not identify with the  political party and say they're  independent and we actually asked  specifically about a very unconventional  type of presidential candidate asking  would you support a presidential  candidate who identified as economically  conservative but also socially liberal  and nearly half said yes that they would  favor that kind of presidential  candidate so all of those things point  to people being open-minded about the  kinds of presidential candidates they're  willing to consider did you ask any  open-ended questions regarding who they  would support in 2012 yes we allowed  them to volunteer any candidate they  would like to see be President or run  for president in 2012 and you know of  course we got a good five percent of  people saying themselves but a few  interesting things point stuck out one  was that about seven percent said they  would like Hillary Clinton to run for  president we also saw a showing for  chris Christie he actually garnered more  votes than john huntsman who is  clared presidential candidate in this  race what does the pole tell us about  the current political landscape that's a  really interesting question and  typically in most polls people ask you  know how do you self-identify your  political views are you a liberal or are  you a conservative so we we do have the  Liberals and the Conservatives that are  economically and socially liberal and  economically and socially conservative  but they actually only make up about  twenty-eight percent each respectively  we've got two other groups though one is  a kind of a small government group kind  of a group of libertarian leaners these  are people who want small government  when it comes to economic issues and  small government when it comes to social  issues as well and they make up about a  quarter of the political electorate the  fourth group we could call  communitarians there are people who  would prefer government to be involved  both in economic and social issues as  well thank you so much for talking to me  thanks for having me I'm Michelle fields  from reason TV
 

